	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == 0 && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == 0 && New_arrListCount >= 1, "The index of the added item is not valid.");

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that new array contains item
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == true, "The new array does not contain the item.");
            
        } 
    }
}
```

In this test, we assume an instance of 'ArrayList' and an integer 'x'. Then we save the state of the array before and after the 'Add' operation. After the operation, a number of assertions are performed to verify that the 'Add' operation was successful. We ensure that the new index and last index of the added element are zero (since the element has been added at the beginning, hence its position is 0) and the count of the array is greater or equal to 1. The 'Contains' method is also checked to verify that the array now contains the element added. 

This test, combined with assumptions, guarantees (under the assumption that the Add's implementation doesn't change in future versions), shows how we can write a test using PexAssumeUnderTest on an ArrayList or List in Pex. 

More detailed tests like these are useful because they exercise a method on its boundaries and can detect issues from the edge of the input space, which might not be covered by more narrow or complicated tests. For example, Adding an item to an empty list results in a list with one item, and a list with one item when we add the same item again contains two items, thus showing functionality beyond the standard use case.

Note: Testing on real scenarios, especially when the software under test is complex like ArrayList is, gets tricky fast. So if feasible, focus more on those areas which are easy to reason about or implement some dummy/random tests to check the basic functionality of all methods. And always refer to the Software Requirements Specification and any project management constraints. The project milestone can also help you determine when to stop testing.

