In this contract test, the `PUT_AddContract` method takes as input parameters an `ArrayList` object (representing the array list) and an integer `x`. It assumes the given `ArrayList` object and `x` as preconditions. It then computes the old and new values and performs various assertions to verify if the `ArrayList`'s `Add` method complies with the specifications of the `List<T>` interface.

The assertions in the test check if the count of the list increases by one after an item is added, the last index of `x` in the list is greater or equal than the old list size, and the list contains `x` and the last index where `x` was found is the index of the added element.

When the `x` parameter is an integer, the condition in the contract test is met because an Integer can always be greater or equal to the size of an ArrayList. Thus, the contract test for the `Add` method is successful.

Here is the same test using the `PexSymbolicValue`:

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
       [PexMethod]
       public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexSymbolicValue(1)]int x)
        {
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int OLD_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int OLD_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool OLD_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.Add(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int NEW_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int NEW_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool NEW_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(NEW_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount+1);
            Debug.Assert(NEW_arrListLastIndexOfX.IsGreaterThanOrEqual(Old_arrListCount));
            Debug.Assert(NEW_arrListContainsX == true && NEW_arrListIndexOfX == OLD_arrListLastIndexOfX + 1);
        }
    }
}
```
The `PUT_AddContract` method now uses PexSymbolicValue to ensure it works for all possible integer values, not just 1. This test is more exhaustive and can verify that the `ArrayList` class satisfies the requirements for the `IList` interface's `Add` method. The test includes all the key properties that are relevant to the `Add` method, including the count, the index of x in the array, whether the array contains x, and the last index where x is found. Testing with symbolic values makes the contract test more general and robust against different types of integer input.

## Test Driven Development (TDD)
Test-Driven Development (TDD) is a development process that relies on the repetition of a very short development cycle: first the developer writes an (initially failing) automated test case, then produces code to make the test pass, and then refactors the code <b>until the test passes</b>. This is in contrast to the test-first approach often used in many other programming paradigms that is based on writing all tests before writing the actual code.

Pex is a tool built on TDD principles that utilizes automated test generation with the goal of testing software against its contracts. The "Contracted" approach involves writing "before" and "after" contract test methods for each public method of a target system.

Before you begin writing actual tests, or test methods, first focus on writing contract test methods. These "contract tests" are like an informal specification of what a function or a method should do--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret >= Old_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}

```

Here is a brief explanation. We are examining all the pertinent data before (`Old_...`) and after (`New_...`) an object has been added to this ArrayList.

In our test, we first assume that the ArrayList and the integer `x` follow all the necessary preconditions (for Add method). And then we observe changes and data across before and after (`Old_...` & `New_...`).

After that, we use our assertions on the changes. We test whether the returned value from the `Add()` method (`New_Ret`) is greater than or equal to the old size of the ArrayList (`Old_arrListCount`).

The assertions can be added or modified as per the requirement of the testing code. For example, you may want to add additional checks for the specific functionalities of the tested code.

For the purpose of this example, I have used the `Assert.IsTrue` condition that checks whether `New_Ret` is greater than or equal to `Old_arrListCount`, which is a simple way to test if an item was successfully added to an ArrayList.

Please test carefully and modify your tests according to your requirements. The Assert class and additional methods from this namespace such as IsFalse, IsNull, IsNotNull, etc., provide other assertion methods that you could use depending upon your needs. They are quite useful when writing tests.

