## ArrayList.Test(v2).cs
```C#
using NUnit.Framework;
using System.Collections;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture]
    public class ArrayListTest
    {
        [Test]
        public void TestArrayListAdd()
        {
            ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
            Assert.AreEqual(0, list.Count);

            list.Add(1);
            Assert.AreEqual(1, list.Count);

            list.Add(2);
            Assert.AreEqual(2, list.Count);

            Assert.AreEqual(1, list[0]);
            Assert.AreEqual(2, list[1]);
        }
    }
}
```

## Explanation of tests

### Assert.AreEqual and Assert.AreNotEqual

These methods are used to verify that the expected result matches the actual result. If the two results do not match, the test will fail.

### TestArrayListAdd

This test verifies the functionality of the Add method of ArrayList. 

To execute the test, we create an instance of ArrayList and add two elements to it (1 and 2) using the Add method. After adding the items, we assert that the Count property of the list is equal to the number of items we added. 

Since the Add method appends the item to the end of the list, we check the first and second items of the list to ensure they contain the expected values. If any of these assertions fail, the test will fail, indicating that the Add method did not work correctly. If all assertions pass, it indicates that the Add method is functioning correctly.

Test case analysis:
This test case adds two elements to the ArrayList, checking the Count property to confirm that it increases by 1 after each addition. The Add--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListCount && New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
